secure_boot_status, on the extracted code.

Charon cannot take an inherent method as an entry point, so the crate root
carries a free function per method and those are the names the manifest counts.
A wrapper that quietly did something other than call through would make every
theorem about the method a statement about code nobody runs, so each one is
proven to be its method below.

These are generated by tools/extraction/mirror_crate.py and are the floor, not
the point. The properties that matter go beneath them, and the shapes most of
them take are stated once in NonosExtraction.Shapes.
-/

import NonosExtraction.UefiSecureBootStatus

open Aeneas Aeneas.Std Result
open nonos_x_uefi_secure_boot_status

set_option linter.hashCommand false
set_option maxRecDepth 100000

namespace NonosExtraction.UefiSecureBootStatus

/-! ### The forwarding functions add nothing -/

theorem the_securebootstatus_is_fully_configured_wrapper_is_its_method (a : secure_boot_status.SecureBootStatus) :
    securebootstatus_is_fully_configured a = secure_boot_status.SecureBootStatus.is_fully_configured a := rfl

theorem the_securebootstatus_can_modify_keys_wrapper_is_its_method (a : secure_boot_status.SecureBootStatus) :
    securebootstatus_can_modify_keys a = secure_boot_status.SecureBootStatus.can_modify_keys a := rfl

/-! ### Axiom profile -/

#print axioms NonosExtraction.UefiSecureBootStatus.the_securebootstatus_is_fully_configured_wrapper_is_its_method
#print axioms NonosExtraction.UefiSecureBootStatus.the_securebootstatus_can_modify_keys_wrapper_is_its_method

end NonosExtraction.UefiSecureBootStatus
